 "title": "2 Ways to Handle Too Expensive",
 "topic": "sales",
 "summary": "The creator explains two directions to handle a prospect who says your offer is too expensive. The main lesson is to explore the customer's reasoning or comparison before trying to justify your pricing.",
 "key_points": [
  "Direction 1: Find out why they think it is expensive by asking: 'Is there any specific reason you believe this is more expensive?'",
  "Uncover their anchor and help them understand why your pricing differs (e.g. condition, year, or mileage).",
  "Get them to give you ammunition to answer before you start justifying your price.",
  "Direction 2: Ask what they are comparing it to by saying: 'Expensive compared to what exactly?'",
  "If they compare it to another quote, explore that quote and see what it gives versus what you are giving.",
  "Alternative response: Tell the customer you can match the price, but you will have to remove features A, B, C, and D, and ask if they are okay with that."
 ],
 "content": "2 ways to handle Too Expensive. Listen. Acknowledge without agreeing. Explore. Is there any specific reason you believe this is more expensive? Direction 1 - Find out why they think it's expensive. When somebody says expensive, here, you can take two directions. Is there any specific reason you believe this is more expensive? If I go to somebody and be like, oh, the car is expensive. Is there any particular reason why you believe this is expensive? Oh, it's expensive because somebody else is giving me the same car for lesser price. Can I ask you a few questions? Is that car in the same condition as this car? It's the same condition, amazing. You mean the same year? And it's run the same number of miles? No, no, it's more miles, year is like, probably a year behind. Oh! You need to find their anchor and then help them understand there is a reason why it is more expensive. But before you start justifying, you want them to give you ammunition to answer that. Direction 2 - Ask what they're comparing it to. Second direction you can take. Hey, can I ask you something? Expensive compared to what exactly? If it is compared to another quote, your job is to explore the quote and see what that quote is giving and what you are giving. The alternative to that, be like, listen, I can give you the same price, but I will have to take off A, B, C and D. Are you okay with that? Another direction you can go, is there any particular reason you didn't go with that quote, and you're speaking to us?",
